.TH TH8 1 "July 3, 2026" "TH8" "TH8 Language Reference"
.SH NAME
th8 \- TH8 scripting language reference
.SH SYNOPSIS
TH8 is a minimal, embeddable Tcl-compatible scripting language
implementing the Tcl Language Standard (Version 1.0).
It provides the Tcl 8.4 surface syntax with strict UTF-8 strings,
a non-recursive evaluation engine, first-class value/token
unification, and a platform abstraction layer that eliminates all
direct C runtime dependencies.
.PP
The engine identifier is
.BR TH8 ,
reported by
.BR "::tcl_platform(engine)" .
.SH "SHELL OPTIONS"
The
.B th8sh
executable accepts the following command-line options.  All option
parsing is performed before any TH8 initialisation, so the options
work even on a half-broken installation.
.TP
.BR \-\-version ", " \-version ", " \-V
Print the build's version, source-control identity (commit hash,
timestamp, tags, VCS), and Tcl-compatibility level to standard
output and exit with status zero.  No interpreter is created; no
script is sourced.  Output is one
.IR "label: value"
pair per line.
.TP
.BR \-eval " \fIscript\fR"
Evaluate the literal string
.I script
as a TH8 program and exit with the script's return code.  Remaining
arguments are placed in the global
.B argv
list.
.TP
.IR script-path " ?" arg " ...?"
Execute the named file as a TH8 script.  Remaining arguments are
placed in the global
.BR argv .
With no arguments, the shell enters an interactive REPL.
.SH "LEXICAL RULES"
TH8 scripts follow the eleven rules of the Tcl(n) manual page
from Tcl 8.4.  In summary:
.IP \(bu 3
A script is a sequence of commands separated by newlines or
semicolons.
.IP \(bu 3
Each command consists of words separated by whitespace (not
newlines).
.IP \(bu 3
Words may be grouped with double quotes (substitutions performed)
or braces (no substitutions except backslash-newline).
.IP \(bu 3
Three forms of substitution:
.BR "$" variable ,
.BR "[" command "]" ,
and backslash sequences.
.IP \(bu 3
Substitutions are performed left to right, exactly once per
character.  Results do not affect word boundaries.
.IP \(bu 3
.B #
at command position begins a comment through end of line.
.SH "BACKSLASH SUBSTITUTION"
.TS
l l.
\fB\\a\fR	Audible alert (U+0007)
\fB\\b\fR	Backspace (U+0008)
\fB\\f\fR	Form feed (U+000C)
\fB\\n\fR	Newline (U+000A)
\fB\\r\fR	Carriage return (U+000D)
\fB\\t\fR	Horizontal tab (U+0009)
\fB\\v\fR	Vertical tab (U+000B)
\fB\\\\\fR	Backslash (U+005C)
\fB\\\fInewline ws\fR	Single space (continuation)
\fB\\x\fIhh\fR	8-bit hex (last 2 digits used)
\fB\\u\fIhhhh\fR	16-bit Unicode (1\-4 hex digits)
\fB\\U\fIhhhhhhhh\fR	21-bit Unicode (1\-8 hex digits)
\fB\\\fIooo\fR	8-bit octal (1\-3 digits)
\fB\\\fIc\fR	Literal character \fIc\fR
.TE
.SH "RETURN CODES"
Every command returns an integer code:
.TP
.B TH8_OK (0)
Normal completion.
.TP
.B TH8_ERROR (1)
Error.  The interpreter result is the error message.
.TP
.B TH8_RETURN (2)
Return from the current procedure.
.TP
.B TH8_BREAK (3)
Break from the innermost loop.
.TP
.B TH8_CONTINUE (4)
Skip to the next iteration of the innermost loop.
.SH "VARIABLE SUBSTITUTION FORMS"
Three forms of variable substitution are recognized:
.TP
.BI $ name
\fIname\fR is composed of letters, digits, underscores, or
namespace separators
.RB ( :: ).
.TP
.BI $ name ( index )
Array element.
\fIindex\fR is subject to command, variable, and backslash
substitution.
.TP
.BI ${ name }
\fIname\fR may contain any character except close brace.
.PP
If the variable does not exist, the substitution is an error.
.SH "LIST REPRESENTATION"
A TH8 list is a string encoding an ordered sequence of elements.
Elements are separated by whitespace.
Elements containing whitespace or special characters are enclosed
in braces or escaped with backslashes.
The empty string is a valid list with zero elements.
.PP
Special characters requiring quoting:
space, tab, newline, carriage return, form feed, vertical tab,
.BR { ", " } ", " \e ", " \(dq ", " [ ", " ] ", " ; ", " $ .
.PP
If the first element starts with
.BR # ,
it is brace-quoted to prevent comment interpretation.
.SH "BUILT-IN COMMANDS"
.SS "CONTROL FLOW"
.TP
\fBbreak\fR
Terminates the innermost enclosing
.BR for ,
.BR foreach ,
or
.B while
loop.
Returns TH8_BREAK.
.TP
\fBcatch\fR \fIscript\fR ?\fIvarname\fR?
Evaluates \fIscript\fR.
If \fIvarname\fR is given, the result of the script (whether
error or success) is stored in the named variable.
Returns the return code of \fIscript\fR as an integer string.
Always returns TH8_OK itself.
.TP
\fBcontinue\fR
Skips to the next iteration of the innermost enclosing loop.
Returns TH8_CONTINUE.
.TP
\fBconcat\fR ?\fIarg ...\fR?
Trims leading and trailing whitespace from each argument, then
joins the trimmed arguments with single spaces.
Interior whitespace within each argument is preserved.
Arguments that are empty after trimming are silently omitted.
With no arguments, returns the empty string.
.TP
\fBerror\fR \fImessage\fR ?\fIinfo\fR? ?\fIcode\fR?
Raises an error.
If \fIinfo\fR is given, it is stored in
.BR ::errorInfo .
If \fIcode\fR is given, it is stored in
.BR ::errorCode .
Returns TH8_ERROR.
.TP
\fBeval\fR \fIarg\fR ?\fIarg ...\fR?
Concatenates all arguments with single spaces and evaluates the
result as a script in the current scope.
Returns the result and return code of the evaluated script.
.TP
\fBexit\fR ?\fIcode\fR?
Set the interpreter exit flag.  All subsequent command evaluations
fail immediately, unwinding the call stack to the host.  If
.I code
is given, it must be an integer; it is emitted to standard error
but does not affect the process exit code.  The flag can only be
cleared by the C API function
.BR Th8_ResetExit .
.TP
\fBfor\fR \fIinit\fR \fItest\fR \fInext\fR \fIbody\fR
Evaluates \fIinit\fR once, then repeatedly:
evaluates \fItest\fR as an expression; if false (zero), stops;
evaluates \fIbody\fR; evaluates \fInext\fR.
.B break
in \fIbody\fR terminates the loop.
.B continue
in \fIbody\fR skips to \fInext\fR.
.TP
\fBforeach\fR \fIvarlist\fR \fIlist\fR \fIbody\fR
\fIvarlist\fR is a list of variable names.
For each group of \fIn\fR values from \fIlist\fR (where \fIn\fR
is the length of \fIvarlist\fR), assigns each value to the
corresponding variable and evaluates \fIbody\fR.
Supports \fBbreak\fR and \fBcontinue\fR.
.TP
\fBif\fR \fIexpr\fR ?\fBthen\fR? \fIbody\fR ?\fBelseif\fR \fIexpr\fR ?\fBthen\fR? \fIbody\fR? ... ?\fBelse\fR \fIbody\fR?
Evaluates each \fIexpr\fR in order as an expression.
The first true (non-zero) condition causes evaluation of its
corresponding \fIbody\fR.
The \fBthen\fR keyword is optional.
If no condition is true and an \fBelse\fR clause exists, its
\fIbody\fR is evaluated.
.TP
\fBreturn\fR ?\fIoptions\fR? ?\fIvalue\fR?
Returns from the current procedure.
Options:
.BR \-code " \fIcode\fR \(em"
set the return code (integer or symbolic:
.BR ok / error / return / break / continue ).
.BR \-errorinfo " \fIinfo\fR \(em"
set the initial
.B ::errorInfo
stack trace.
.BR \-errorcode " \fIcode\fR \(em"
set
.BR ::errorCode .
If \fIvalue\fR is given, it becomes the interpreter result;
otherwise the result is the empty string.
.TP
\fBswitch\fR ?\fIoptions\fR? \fIstring\fR \fIpattern\fR \fIbody\fR ?\fIpattern\fR \fIbody\fR ...?
Compares \fIstring\fR against each \fIpattern\fR in order.
Options:
.BR \-exact " (default),"
.BR \-glob ", " \-regexp ", " \-\- .
With
.BR \-regexp ,
patterns are treated as regular expressions (requires the
regexp module).
The special pattern
.B default
matches any string (must be last).
If a \fIbody\fR is the literal string
.BR \- ,
evaluation falls through to the next body.
.TP
\fBwhile\fR \fItest\fR \fIbody\fR
Repeatedly evaluates \fItest\fR as an expression; if true
(non-zero), evaluates \fIbody\fR.
Supports \fBbreak\fR and \fBcontinue\fR.
.TP
\fBcoroutine\fR \fIname\fR \fIcommand\fR ?\fIarg ...\fR?
Creates a new coroutine named \fIname\fR.  The \fIcommand\fR is
evaluated with the given arguments.  When the command calls
.BR yield ,
execution suspends and the yield value is returned to the caller.
The coroutine name becomes a command; invoking it resumes the
coroutine.  When the body completes, the coroutine command is
automatically deleted and the body's result is returned.
.TP
\fByield\fR ?\fIvalue\fR?
Suspends the current coroutine and returns \fIvalue\fR (default:
empty string) to the code that resumed the coroutine.  When the
coroutine is next resumed, \fByield\fR returns the value passed
by the resume call.  It is an error to call \fByield\fR outside
a coroutine body.
.TP
\fBtry\fR \fIscript\fR ?\fBfinally\fR \fIscript\fR?
Evaluates \fIscript\fR and preserves its result and return code.
If a
.B finally
clause is present, evaluates the finally script after the body
completes, regardless of success or failure.
If the finally script succeeds, the body's result is returned.
If the finally script fails, its error overrides the body's result.
.RS
.PP
The finally script is temporarily exempt from a pending script
cancellation: the cancel state is saved before the finally block
and restored after it completes.  The finally script is NOT
evaluated if the interpreter's exit flag is set.  The finally
block receives a fresh memory allocation budget equal to the
interpreter's limit (maximum total memory during finally is 2x
the configured limit).
.PP
Unlike Tcl 8.6's
.BR try ,
there are no
.BR catch ", " on ", or " trap
clauses.  Use
.B catch
separately for error handling.  This is the purest form of
.BR try / finally ,
adapted from Eagle.
.RE
.SS "VARIABLES"
.TP
\fBappend\fR \fIvarname\fR ?\fIvalue ...\fR?
Appends each \fIvalue\fR to the variable \fIvarname\fR.
Creates the variable if it does not exist.
Returns the new value.
.TP
\fBarray exists\fR \fIvarname\fR
Returns
.B 1
if \fIvarname\fR is an array variable,
.B 0
otherwise.
.TP
\fBarray get\fR \fIvarname\fR ?\fIpattern\fR?
Returns a list of name-value pairs for all elements of the
array.  If \fIpattern\fR is given, only elements whose names
match the glob pattern are included.  Returns an empty list if
the variable does not exist or is not an array.
.TP
\fBarray names\fR \fIvarname\fR
Returns a list of all element names in the array \fIvarname\fR.
Returns an empty list if the variable does not exist or is not
an array.
.TP
\fBarray set\fR \fIvarname\fR \fIlist\fR
Sets array elements from a name-value pair \fIlist\fR.  It is
an error if \fIlist\fR has an odd number of elements.  An empty
list creates an empty array variable if it does not already exist.
.TP
\fBarray size\fR \fIvarname\fR
Returns the number of elements in the array.  Returns 0 if
the variable does not exist or is not an array.
.TP
\fBglobal\fR \fIvarname\fR ?\fIvarname ...\fR?
Within a procedure, creates local variables linked to the
corresponding variables in the global namespace.
Equivalent to
.BI "upvar #0 " "varname varname"
for each argument.
Outside a procedure, has no effect.
.TP
\fBvariable\fR \fIname\fR ?\fIvalue\fR? ?\fIname value ...\fR?
Within a namespace body, declares each \fIname\fR as a
namespace-scoped variable and optionally initializes it to
\fIvalue\fR.  Inside a procedure, links each \fIname\fR to the
same-named variable in the enclosing namespace (create-on-read
if it does not exist).  Returns the empty string.  See
.BR namespace .
.TP
\fBincr\fR \fIvarname\fR ?\fIincrement\fR?
Adds \fIincrement\fR (default 1) to the integer value of
\fIvarname\fR.
Creates the variable with value \fIincrement\fR if it does not
exist.
Returns the new value.
.TP
\fBset\fR \fIvarname\fR ?\fIvalue\fR?
If \fIvalue\fR is given, sets \fIvarname\fR to \fIvalue\fR.
Returns the value of \fIvarname\fR.
If reading and the variable does not exist, returns an error.
.TP
\fBunset\fR ?\fB\-nocomplain\fR? ?\fB\-\-\fR? ?\fIvarname\fR ...?
Removes each named variable from the current scope.
Returns an error if a variable does not exist (unless
\fB\-nocomplain\fR is specified).  The \fB\-\-\fR option marks
the end of options, allowing variable names that begin with
a dash.  With no variable names, the command is a no-op.
.TP
\fBuplevel\fR ?\fIlevel\fR? \fIscript\fR
Evaluates \fIscript\fR in the stack frame identified by
\fIlevel\fR.
If \fIlevel\fR is omitted, defaults to 1 (the caller's frame).
\fIlevel\fR may be a positive integer (relative) or
.BI # n
(absolute from bottom).
.TP
\fBdownlevel\fR \fIscript\fR
Evaluates \fIscript\fR in the call frame that was active just
prior to the most recent
.BR uplevel .
This undoes the scope change of
.BR uplevel ,
running the script back in the frame the uplevel departed from.
Both the variable scope and the namespace context of the target
frame are restored for the duration of the evaluation.
It is an error to call
.B downlevel
when no
.B uplevel
is currently active.
.TP
\fBupvar\fR ?\fIlevel\fR? \fIothervar\fR \fImyvar\fR ?\fIothervar\fR \fImyvar\fR ...?
Creates local variables (\fImyvar\fR) linked to variables
(\fIothervar\fR) in the frame identified by \fIlevel\fR
(default 1).
.SS "EXPRESSIONS"
.TP
\fBexpr\fR \fIexpression\fR
Evaluates \fIexpression\fR and returns the result.
Operators (highest precedence first):
.PP
.RS
.TS
l l l.
\fB\- + ~ !\fR	unary	right
\fB**\fR	exponentiation	right
\fB* / %\fR	multiplicative	left
\fB+ \-\fR	additive	left
\fB<< >>\fR	shift	left
\fB< > <= >=\fR	comparison	left
\fB== !=\fR	numeric equality	left
\fBeq ne\fR	string equality	left
\fB&\fR	bitwise AND	left
\fB^\fR	bitwise XOR	left
\fB|\fR	bitwise OR	left
\fB&&\fR	logical AND (short-circuit)	left
\fB||\fR	logical OR (short-circuit)	left
\fB? :\fR	ternary (short-circuit)	right
.TE
.RE
.PP
.RS
Integer literals: decimal, \fB0x\fR hex, \fB0o\fR octal,
\fB0b\fR binary.
A leading zero does \fBnot\fR imply octal.
.PP
Math functions (60 total):
.PP
Core:
.BR abs ", " bool ", " double ", " entier ", " epsilon ", "
.BR int ", " max ", " min ", " rand ", " round ", "
.BR srand ", " typeof ", " wide .
.PP
Transcendental:
.BR acos ", " asin ", " atan ", " atan2 ", " ceil ", "
.BR cos ", " cosh ", " exp ", " floor ", " fmod ", "
.BR hypot ", " log ", " log10 ", " pow ", " sin ", "
.BR sinh ", " sqrt ", " tan ", " tanh .
.PP
C99:
.BR acosh ", " asinh ", " atanh ", " cbrt ", " copysign ", "
.BR dim ", " erf ", " erfc ", " exp2 ", " expm1 ", "
.BR gamma ", " ldexp ", " lgamma ", " log1p ", " log2 ", "
.BR logb ", " nextafter ", " remainder ", " signbit ", "
.BR trunc .
.PP
Classification (TIP #521):
.BR fpclassify ", " isfinite ", " isinf ", " isnan ", "
.BR isnormal ", " issubnormal ", " isunordered .
.RE
.TP
\fBfpclassify\fR \fInumber\fR
Classifies the floating-point
.I number
and returns one of
.BR zero ,
.BR subnormal ,
.BR normal ,
.BR infinite ,
or
.BR nan .
The command form is equivalent to the
.B fpclassify(x)
expression math function and matches its result exactly (see
.BR expr ).
.SS "LISTS"
.TP
\fBjoin\fR \fIlist\fR ?\fIseparator\fR?
Returns a string formed by joining the elements of \fIlist\fR
with \fIseparator\fR (default: single space).
.TP
\fBlappend\fR \fIvarname\fR \fIvalue\fR ?\fIvalue ...\fR?
Appends each \fIvalue\fR as a new element to the list stored
in \fIvarname\fR.
Creates the variable if it does not exist.
Returns the new list value.
.TP
\fBlindex\fR \fIlist\fR ?\fIindex\fR ...?
Returns an element from \fIlist\fR.  With no \fIindex\fR, returns
the list unchanged (identity).  With one index, returns the element
at that position.  With multiple indices, performs nested indexing
into sublists.  A single argument that is itself a list of indices
(e.g., \fB{1 0}\fR) is equivalent to multiple index arguments.
Indices are zero-based.
.B end
refers to the last element;
.BI end\- n
refers to the element \fIn\fR positions before the end.
Returns the empty string if out of range.
.TP
\fBlinsert\fR \fIlist\fR \fIindex\fR ?\fIelement ...\fR?
Returns a new list with the given elements inserted before
position \fIindex\fR in \fIlist\fR.  Supports
.B end
and
.BI end\- n
indices.
.TP
\fBlist\fR ?\fIarg ...\fR?
Returns a properly formed list whose elements are the given
arguments.
.TP
\fBllength\fR \fIlist\fR
Returns the number of elements in \fIlist\fR.
.TP
\fBlrange\fR \fIlist\fR \fIfirst\fR \fIlast\fR
Returns a new list containing elements from position \fIfirst\fR
to \fIlast\fR (inclusive).  Supports
.B end
and
.BI end\- n
indices.
.TP
\fBlreplace\fR \fIlist\fR \fIfirst\fR \fIlast\fR ?\fIelement ...\fR?
Returns a new list with elements from \fIfirst\fR to \fIlast\fR
replaced by the given elements.  If no replacement elements are
given, the range is simply deleted.
.TP
\fBlsearch\fR ?\fIoptions\fR? \fIlist\fR \fIpattern\fR
Returns the index of the first matching element, or
.B \-1
if no match.
Options:
.BR \-exact " (exact string comparison),"
.BR \-glob " (glob matching, default),"
.BR \-regexp " (regular expression matching),"
.BR \-all " (return all matching indices),"
.BR \-inline " (return values instead of indices),"
.BR \-not " (negate the match),"
.BR \-start " \fIn\fR (begin at index \fIn\fR)."
.TP
\fBlsort\fR ?\fIoptions\fR? \fIlist\fR
Returns a sorted copy of \fIlist\fR.
The
.B \-command
option specifies a comparison script that receives two elements and returns
an integer.  The
.B \-index
option sorts by the Nth sub-element of each list element.  The
.B \-dictionary
option uses case-insensitive comparison with embedded integers compared
numerically.
.TP
\fBsplit\fR \fIstring\fR ?\fIsplitchars\fR?
Returns a list formed by splitting \fIstring\fR at each
occurrence of any character in \fIsplitchars\fR.
If \fIsplitchars\fR is omitted, splits on whitespace.
If \fIsplitchars\fR is the empty string, splits into
individual characters (code points).
Adjacent split characters produce empty-string elements.
.TP
\fBlreverse\fR \fIlist\fR
Returns a new list with the elements of \fIlist\fR in reverse
order.  An empty list returns an empty string.
.TP
\fBlremove\fR \fIlist\fR ?\fIindex ...\fR?
Returns \fIlist\fR with the elements at the given indices
removed.  Indices may be integers,
.BR end ", or"
.BI end\- n\fR.
Out-of-range or duplicate indices are silently ignored.
With no indices, returns the original list unchanged.
.TP
\fBlassign\fR \fIlist\fR ?\fIvarname ...\fR?
Assigns successive elements of \fIlist\fR to the named variables.
If there are more variables than list elements, excess variables
are set to the empty string.  Returns a list of unassigned elements
(those beyond the last variable).  With no variable names, returns
the original list.
.TP
\fBdict create\fR ?\fIkey value ...\fR?
Returns a new dictionary from the given key-value pairs.
It is an error to provide an odd number of arguments.
.TP
\fBdict get\fR \fIdictionary\fR ?\fIkey ...\fR?
Returns the value for the given key path.  With no keys, returns
the entire dictionary.  With multiple keys, performs nested lookup.
.TP
\fBdict set\fR \fIdictVarName\fR \fIkey\fR ?\fIkey ...\fR? \fIvalue\fR
Sets a value in the dictionary variable at the given key path.
Creates the variable and intermediate dictionaries as needed.
.TP
\fBdict unset\fR \fIdictVarName\fR \fIkey\fR ?\fIkey ...\fR?
Removes the entry at the given key path from the dictionary variable.
.TP
\fBdict exists\fR \fIdictionary\fR \fIkey\fR ?\fIkey ...\fR?
Returns 1 if the key path exists in the dictionary, 0 otherwise.
.TP
\fBdict keys\fR \fIdictionary\fR ?\fIpattern\fR?
Returns a list of all keys in the dictionary.  If \fIpattern\fR is
given, only keys matching the glob pattern are returned.
.TP
\fBdict values\fR \fIdictionary\fR ?\fIpattern\fR?
Returns a list of all values in the dictionary.  If \fIpattern\fR is
given, only values whose keys match the pattern are returned.
.TP
\fBdict size\fR \fIdictionary\fR
Returns the number of key-value pairs in the dictionary.
.TP
\fBdict for\fR \fB{\fR\fIkeyVar valueVar\fR\fB}\fR \fIdictionary\fR \fIbody\fR
Iterates over each key-value pair, setting \fIkeyVar\fR and
\fIvalueVar\fR before evaluating \fIbody\fR.
Supports \fBbreak\fR and \fBcontinue\fR.
.TP
\fBdict filter\fR \fIdictionary\fR \fBkey\fR \fIpattern\fR
Returns a new dictionary containing only entries whose key
matches the glob \fIpattern\fR.
.TP
\fBdict filter\fR \fIdictionary\fR \fBvalue\fR \fIpattern\fR
Returns a new dictionary containing only entries whose value
matches the glob \fIpattern\fR.
.TP
\fBdict filter\fR \fIdictionary\fR \fBscript\fR \fB{\fR\fIkeyVar valueVar\fR\fB}\fR \fIscript\fR
Returns a new dictionary containing only entries for which
\fIscript\fR evaluates to true.
.TP
\fBdict merge\fR ?\fIdictionary ...\fR?
Merges one or more dictionaries, with later dictionaries
overriding earlier ones for duplicate keys.
.TP
\fBdict remove\fR \fIdictionary\fR ?\fIkey ...\fR?
Returns a copy of \fIdictionary\fR with the named keys removed.
.TP
\fBdict replace\fR \fIdictionary\fR ?\fIkey value ...\fR?
Returns a copy of \fIdictionary\fR with the given key-value pairs
added or replaced.
.TP
\fBdict append\fR \fIdictVarName\fR \fIkey\fR ?\fIstring ...\fR?
Appends \fIstring\fR(s) to the value for \fIkey\fR in the
dictionary variable.
.TP
\fBdict lappend\fR \fIdictVarName\fR \fIkey\fR ?\fIvalue ...\fR?
Appends \fIvalue\fR(s) as list elements to the value for \fIkey\fR.
.TP
\fBdict incr\fR \fIdictVarName\fR \fIkey\fR ?\fIincrement\fR?
Increments the value for \fIkey\fR by \fIincrement\fR (default 1).
.TP
\fBdict map\fR \fB{\fR\fIkeyVar valueVar\fR\fB}\fR \fIdictionary\fR \fIbody\fR
Like \fBdict for\fR, but collects the body results into a new
dictionary keyed by \fIkeyVar\fR.
.TP
\fBdict update\fR \fIdictVarName\fR \fIkey\fR \fIvarName\fR ?\fIkey\fR \fIvarName\fR ...? \fIbody\fR
Extracts keys from the dictionary variable into local variables,
evaluates \fIbody\fR, then writes changes back.
.TP
\fBdict with\fR \fIdictVarName\fR ?\fIkey ...\fR? \fIbody\fR
Like \fBdict update\fR, but exposes all keys as variables.
.TP
\fBdict info\fR \fIdictionary\fR
Returns diagnostic information about the dictionary (number of
entries).
.SS "STRINGS"
.TP
\fBstring compare\fR ?\fB\-nocase\fR? ?\fB\-length\fR \fIn\fR? \fIstr1\fR \fIstr2\fR
Returns
.BR \-1 ", " 0 ", or " 1
depending on whether \fIstr1\fR is less than, equal to, or
greater than \fIstr2\fR in Unicode code point order.
If \fB\-nocase\fR is given, comparison is case-insensitive.
If \fB\-length\fR is given, at most \fIn\fR characters are compared.
.TP
\fBstring equal\fR ?\fB\-nocase\fR? ?\fB\-length\fR \fIn\fR? \fIstr1\fR \fIstr2\fR
Returns
.B 1
if the strings are equal,
.B 0
otherwise.  Supports the same options as
.BR "string compare" .
.TP
\fBstring first\fR \fIneedle\fR \fIhaystack\fR ?\fIstartIndex\fR?
Returns the character index (zero-based) of the first occurrence
of \fIneedle\fR in \fIhaystack\fR at or after \fIstartIndex\fR
(default 0), or
.B \-1
if not found.
.TP
\fBstring index\fR \fIstring\fR \fIindex\fR
Returns the character at position \fIindex\fR in \fIstring\fR.
Supports
.B end
and
.BI end\- n
indices.
Returns the empty string if out of range.
.TP
\fBstring is\fR \fIclass\fR \fIstring\fR
Returns
.B 1
if every character in \fIstring\fR belongs to \fIclass\fR,
.B 0
otherwise.
Empty strings return
.BR 0 .
Supports \fB\-strict\fR flag.
Classes:
.BR alnum ", " alpha ", " ascii ", " boolean ", "
.BR digit ", " double ", " false ", " integer ", "
.BR list ", " space ", " tainted ", " true ", " xdigit .
.TP
\fBstring last\fR \fIneedle\fR \fIhaystack\fR ?\fIstartIndex\fR?
Returns the character index of the last occurrence of
\fIneedle\fR in \fIhaystack\fR, or
.BR \-1 .
.TP
\fBstring length\fR \fIstring\fR
Returns the number of characters (Unicode code points) in
\fIstring\fR.
.TP
\fBstring map\fR ?\fB\-nocase\fR? \fImapping\fR \fIstring\fR
\fImapping\fR is a list of key-value pairs.
For each key found in \fIstring\fR, substitutes the
corresponding value.
Substitution proceeds left to right; replacement text is not
re-scanned.
.TP
\fBstring match\fR ?\fB\-nocase\fR? \fIpattern\fR \fIstring\fR
Returns
.B 1
if \fIstring\fR matches the glob \fIpattern\fR,
.B 0
otherwise.
Glob metacharacters:
.B *
(any sequence),
.B ?
(any character),
.BI [ chars ]
(character class),
.BI \e x
(literal).
.TP
\fBstring range\fR \fIstring\fR \fIfirst\fR \fIlast\fR
Returns the substring from character \fIfirst\fR to character
\fIlast\fR (inclusive).
Supports
.B end
and
.BI end\- n
indices.
.TP
\fBstring replace\fR \fIstring\fR \fIfirst\fR \fIlast\fR ?\fInewString\fR?
Returns a copy of \fIstring\fR with the characters from \fIfirst\fR to
\fIlast\fR replaced by \fInewString\fR.  If \fInewString\fR is omitted,
the characters in the range are deleted.
.TP
\fBstring repeat\fR \fIstring\fR \fIcount\fR
Returns \fIstring\fR concatenated \fIcount\fR times.
Subject to the result size limit.
.TP
\fBstring trim\fR \fIstring\fR ?\fIchars\fR?
Returns \fIstring\fR with leading and trailing characters in
\fIchars\fR removed.
\fIchars\fR defaults to whitespace.
.TP
\fBstring trimleft\fR \fIstring\fR ?\fIchars\fR?
Returns \fIstring\fR with leading characters in \fIchars\fR
removed.
.TP
\fBstring trimright\fR \fIstring\fR ?\fIchars\fR?
Returns \fIstring\fR with trailing characters in \fIchars\fR
removed.
.TP
\fBstring totitle\fR \fIstring\fR ?\fIfirst\fR? ?\fIlast\fR?
Returns a copy of \fIstring\fR with the first character converted to
uppercase and the remaining characters to lowercase.  If \fIfirst\fR and
\fIlast\fR are given, only the characters in that range are affected.
.TP
\fBsubst\fR ?\fB\-nobackslashes\fR? ?\fB\-nocommands\fR? ?\fB\-novariables\fR? \fIstring\fR
Performs variable, command, and backslash substitution on
.I string
and returns the result.
Flags selectively disable individual substitution types.
Unlike the normal parser, braces have no special quoting
effect inside
.BR subst .
If a command substitution raises an error, the error propagates.
A
.B break
exception stops processing (result is everything so far).
A
.B continue
exception replaces that command with the empty string.
A
.B return
exception substitutes the returned value.
TIP\ #712 positive flags
.RB ( \-backslashes ", " \-commands ", " \-variables )
are also accepted; when at least one positive flag is present,
only the named substitution types are performed.
.TP
\fBformat\fR \fIformatString\fR ?\fIarg ...\fR?
Format
.IR arg s
according to
.IR formatString ,
producing a result string.  Supports the
.BR %d ", " %i ", " %u ", " %o ", " %x ", " %X ", "
.BR %c ", " %s ", " %f ", " %e ", " %E ", " %g ", " %G ", and "
.B %%
conversions, plus the flag characters
.BR \- ", " + ", " 0 ", " # ,
and a space flag;
a numeric or
.B *
width; a precision
.B .N
or
.BR .* ;
and the length modifiers
.BR h " and " l .
Carry-past-leading-digit overflow in the float conversions
(e.g.
.B \(dqformat \(dq%.1f\(dq 9.99\(dq
=>
.BR \(dq10.0\(dq )
is handled correctly across both fixed and scientific
mantissa renormalization.
.TP
\fBscan\fR \fIstring\fR \fIformatString\fR ?\fIvarName ...\fR?
Parse
.I string
according to
.IR formatString ,
which uses
.BR % \-conversion
specifiers analogous to
.BR format .
Supports
.BR %d ", " %i ", " %u ", " %o ", " %x ", " %X ", "
.BR %c ", " %s ", " %f ", " %n ", and "
.BR %% .
When variable names are given, assigns each conversion in
order and returns the number of successful conversions.
With no variable names, returns the conversions as a list.
The
.B %f
scanner accepts an optional
.RB ( + / \- )
sign after the
.B e
or
.B E
exponent
.RB ( \(dq1e\-3\(dq ", " \(dq2.5E+10\(dq ).
.SS "BINARY DATA"
The
.B binary
ensemble packs scripted values into byte sequences and parses
byte sequences back into scripted values.  Format strings are
read left-to-right; each specifier is a letter followed by an
optional non-negative decimal count or the literal
.BR * .
Whitespace inside a format string is ignored.
.TP
\fBbinary format\fR \fIformatString\fR ?\fIarg arg ...\fR?
Returns a byte sequence built by packing each \fIarg\fR
according to its specifier.
Specifiers (\fIN\fR = decimal count, default 1):
.RS
.TP 4
\fBa\fR\fIN\fR / \fBA\fR\fIN\fR
ASCII byte field;
.B a
NUL-pads and
.B A
space-pads to length \fIN\fR.
Input longer than \fIN\fR is truncated.
With count
.BR * ,
the field length equals the input string length (no padding or
truncation).
.TP 4
\fBb\fR\fIN\fR / \fBB\fR\fIN\fR
Bit field of \fIN\fR bits.
.B b
encodes low-bit-first within each byte;
.B B
encodes high-bit-first.
One input character produces one bit:
.B 0
means zero, any other character means one.
With count
.BR * ,
the count equals the input string's character count.
.TP 4
\fBh\fR\fIN\fR / \fBH\fR\fIN\fR
Hex field of \fIN\fR nibbles.
.B h
encodes low-nibble-first within each byte;
.B H
encodes high-nibble-first.
With count
.BR * ,
the count equals the input string's character count.
.TP 4
\fBc\fR\fIN\fR / \fBs\fR\fIN\fR / \fBS\fR\fIN\fR / \fBt\fR\fIN\fR
\fBi\fR\fIN\fR / \fBI\fR\fIN\fR / \fBn\fR\fIN\fR /
\fBw\fR\fIN\fR / \fBW\fR\fIN\fR / \fBm\fR\fIN\fR
Integer fields, 8 / 16 / 16 / 16 / 32 / 32 / 32 / 64 / 64 / 64
bits respectively;
lowercase forms are little-endian,
uppercase forms are big-endian,
.BR t / n / m
are host-native byte order.
\fIN\fR successive values are packed.
Integer values larger than the field width are truncated modulo
.BR "2^width" .
When TH8_ENABLE_BIGINT is enabled at compile time and bigint is
enabled at runtime, bigint inputs are accepted and truncated
to the field width.
With count
.BR * ,
the next argument is treated as a Tcl list and one field is
emitted per list element.
.TP 4
\fBf\fR\fIN\fR / \fBr\fR\fIN\fR / \fBR\fR\fIN\fR
IEEE 754 binary32 (single-precision) field;
.B f
is host-native,
.B r
is little-endian,
.B R
is big-endian.
NaN inputs are normalised to the canonical pattern
.BR 0x7FC00000 .
With count
.BR * ,
the next argument is treated as a Tcl list and one field is
emitted per element.
.TP 4
\fBd\fR\fIN\fR / \fBq\fR\fIN\fR / \fBQ\fR\fIN\fR
IEEE 754 binary64 (double-precision) field;
.B d
is host-native,
.B q
is little-endian,
.B Q
is big-endian.
NaN inputs are normalised to
.BR 0x7FF8000000000000 .
With count
.BR * ,
the next argument is treated as a Tcl list and one field is
emitted per element.
.TP 4
\fBj\fR\fIN\fR / \fBJ\fR\fIN\fR
Arbitrary-precision signed integer field, exactly \fIN\fR
bytes wide, two's-complement encoded.
.B j
writes/reads the bytes in little-endian order;
.B J
writes/reads in big-endian order.
Values outside the range
.RB [ \-2^(N*8\-1) ", " 2^(N*8\-1)\-1 ]
raise the error
.BR \(dqinteger value too large for j/J field\(dq .
With count
.BR * ,
.B binary format
packs the value in the minimum number of bytes needed (one
byte for zero; for non-zero values, just enough to include
the sign bit) and
.B binary scan
consumes all remaining bytes as a single signed bigint
(yielding zero on an empty input).
.B binary scan
always interprets the field as
.B signed
(MSB of the most-significant byte is the sign bit) and
sign-extends.
When
.B TH8_ENABLE_BIGINT
is off at compile time or
.BR Th8_IsBigintEnabled (interp)
is false at runtime, the field is limited to values
representable in a 64-bit two's-complement integer; scans
wider than eight bytes raise an error in that
configuration.
.TP 4
\fBx\fR\fIN\fR
Inserts \fIN\fR NUL bytes (consumes no value argument).
Count
.B *
is not permitted on
.BR x .
.TP 4
\fBX\fR\fIN\fR
Moves the cursor back by \fIN\fR bytes (no value argument);
movement past the start of the buffer is silently clamped to
position zero.
With count
.BR * ,
the cursor rewinds to position zero.
.TP 4
\fB@\fR\fIN\fR
Moves the cursor to absolute byte position \fIN\fR (no value
argument); any gap between the previous high-water mark and
the seek position is zero-filled.
Count
.B *
is not permitted on
.BR @ .
.RE
.IP
Errors:
.B "\(dqnot enough arguments for all format specifiers\(dq"
when the supplied value arguments are insufficient;
.B "\(dqbad field specifier\(dq"
when the format string contains an unknown letter;
.B "\(dqcannot use \e\(dq*\e\(dq in binary format field\(dq"
when
.B *
is used on
.B x
or
.BR @ .
.TP
\fBbinary scan\fR \fIbinaryData formatString\fR ?\fIvarName varName ...\fR?
Parses
.I binaryData
according to
.I formatString
and assigns the parsed values to the named variables.
Returns the number of variables successfully assigned (the
partial-scan count): if
.I binaryData
runs out before a specifier consumes its bytes, scanning stops,
the corresponding variable and any subsequent variables are
left untouched, and the count reflects only the variables
that were assigned.
The same specifier letters and counts apply as for
.B binary format
with these differences:
.RS
.TP 4
\fBa\fR\fIN\fR
Reads exactly \fIN\fR bytes verbatim (no NUL stripping).
.TP 4
\fBA\fR\fIN\fR
Reads \fIN\fR bytes and strips trailing space and NUL bytes.
.TP 4
\fBa\fR\fB*\fR / \fBA\fR\fB*\fR / \fBb\fR\fB*\fR / \fBB\fR\fB*\fR / \fBh\fR\fB*\fR / \fBH\fR\fB*\fR
Reads all remaining bytes (or their bit / nibble equivalent).
.TP 4
\fBc\fR\fB*\fR / \fBs\fR\fB*\fR / ... / \fBd\fR\fB*\fR / \fBq\fR\fB*\fR / \fBQ\fR\fB*\fR
Reads as many fields as remain in the input and assigns the
list of values to a single variable.
.TP 4
\fBx\fR\fIN\fR
Skips \fIN\fR input bytes (no variable consumed).
.RE
.SS "PROCEDURES"
.TP
\fBproc\fR \fIname\fR \fIarglist\fR \fIbody\fR
Creates a new command \fIname\fR.
\fIarglist\fR is a list where each element is either a simple
name (required parameter), a two-element list
.BI { "name default" }
(optional parameter with default value), or the literal
.B args
(must be last; collects all remaining arguments as a list).
When \fIname\fR is invoked, a new frame is pushed, parameters
are bound, and \fIbody\fR is evaluated.
.TP
\fBnproc\fR \fIname\fR \fIarglist\fR \fIbody\fR
Like
.BR proc ,
but uses named/keyword argument binding.  Parameters whose
names start with \fB\-\fR are matched by \fB\-name value\fR
pairs in the call; remaining parameters are filled positionally.
.TP
\fBapply\fR \fIlambda\fR ?\fIarg ...\fR?
Evaluates an anonymous procedure.
.I lambda
is a two\-element list
.BI { "arglist body" } \fR.
A new frame is pushed, arguments are bound per
.BR proc
rules, and
.I body
is evaluated.
.TP
\fBnapply\fR \fIlambda\fR ?\fIarg ...\fR?
Like
.BR apply ,
but uses
.BR nproc \-style
named/keyword argument binding.
.TP
\fBrename\fR \fIoldname\fR \fInewname\fR
Renames the command \fIoldname\fR to \fInewname\fR.
If \fInewname\fR is the empty string, the command is deleted.
.TP
\fBtailcall\fR \fIcommand\fR ?\fIarg ...\fR?
Replaces the current procedure invocation with a call to
\fIcommand\fR.  The current procedure's frame is replaced,
enabling tail\-call optimization for recursive algorithms.
.SS "REGULAR EXPRESSIONS"
.TP
\fBregexp\fR ?\fIswitches\fR? \fIpattern\fR \fIstring\fR ?\fImatchvar\fR? ?\fIsubvar ...\fR?
Matches \fIstring\fR against the regular expression \fIpattern\fR.
Returns 1 if matched, 0 otherwise.  If match variables are given,
the overall match is stored in \fImatchvar\fR and sub-match groups
in subsequent \fIsubvar\fRs.
.RS
.PP
Switches:
.BR \-nocase ", " \-expanded ", " \-line ", " \-linestop ", "
.BR \-lineanchor ", " \-indices ", " \-start " \fIn\fR, " \-\- .
.PP
.B \-all
\(em returns the total number of non-overlapping matches.
.PP
.B \-inline
\(em returns matches as a list instead of storing in variables.
With
.BR \-all ,
collects all matches into a flat list.
.PP
See
.BR th8_re_syntax (1)
for the complete regular expression syntax reference.
.RE
.TP
\fBregsub\fR ?\fIswitches\fR? \fIpattern\fR \fIstring\fR \fIreplacement\fR ?\fIvarname\fR?
Substitutes the first (or all with
.BR \-all )
occurrences of \fIpattern\fR in \fIstring\fR with
\fIreplacement\fR.
In \fIreplacement\fR,
.B &
is the matched text and
.BI \e n
is the \fIn\fRth sub-match.
If \fIvarname\fR is given, the result is stored there and the
command returns a count; otherwise the result is the substituted
string.
.SS "ENCODING"
.TP
\fBbase64 encode\fR \fIstring\fR
Returns the Base64 encoding of \fIstring\fR.
.TP
\fBbase64 decode\fR \fIstring\fR
Returns the decoded data from a Base64-encoded \fIstring\fR.
Invalid Base64 characters are silently ignored.
.SS "I/O"
.TP
\fBgets\fR \fIchannel\fR ?\fIvarname\fR?
Reads available data from \fIchannel\fR.
TH8 supports one input channel:
.BR stdin .
If the returned data ends with a newline, the trailing line
ending is stripped.
.RS
.PP
With \fIvarname\fR: stores the data in the variable and returns
the character count, or
.B \-1
on failure (not an error).
.PP
Without \fIvarname\fR: returns the data directly; failure is
an error.
.RE
.TP
\fBread\fR ?\fB\-nonewline\fR? \fIchannelId\fR
.TQ
\fBread\fR \fIchannelId\fR \fInumChars\fR
Reads data from \fIchannelId\fR.  Two forms are supported
(Tcl 8.6 conformance):
.RS
.PP
\fBread\fR ?\fB\-nonewline\fR? \fIchannelId\fR: reads all data
from the channel until EOF.  With \fB\-nonewline\fR, the trailing
newline character is stripped from the result.
.PP
\fBread\fR \fIchannelId\fR \fInumChars\fR: reads exactly
\fInumChars\fR characters, or fewer if EOF is reached first.
.PP
Supports \fBstdin\fR and temporary file channels.
.RE
.TP
\fBputs\fR ?\fB\-nonewline\fR? ?\fIchannel\fR? \fIstring\fR
Writes \fIstring\fR to the output channel, followed by a
newline unless \fB\-nonewline\fR is specified.
\fIchannel\fR defaults to
.BR stdout .
The only supported channel is
.BR stdout .
The
.B stderr
channel is reserved for system error reporting by the
interpreter and host application; it is not available from
scripts.  Attempting to use any channel other than
.B stdout
produces a \(lqchannel not available\(rq error.
.TP
\fBfile tempname\fR \fIsize\fR
Creates a pre-allocated temporary file of at least \fIsize\fR bytes
and returns the channel name.  The channel is sandboxed: writes may
not exceed the declared size.  The file is deleted when the
interpreter is destroyed.
.TP
\fBfile channels\fR ?\fIpattern\fR?
Returns a list of all open channel names.  When the platform
provides \fBxInput\fR and \fBxOutput\fR callbacks, the list
includes \fBstdin\fR and \fBstdout\fR in addition to any
temporary file channels.
If \fIpattern\fR is given, only matching names are returned.
.TP
\fBseek\fR \fIchannel offset\fR ?\fIorigin\fR?
Sets the read/write position for \fIchannel\fR.
\fIorigin\fR is one of \fBstart\fR (default), \fBcurrent\fR, or
\fBend\fR.  Only temporary file channels are supported.
.TP
\fBtell\fR \fIchannel\fR
Returns the current byte position for \fIchannel\fR.
.TP
\fBflush\fR \fIchannel\fR
Flushes any pending writes for \fIchannel\fR.
.TP
\fBclose\fR ?\fIchannelId\fR?
Closes a temporary channel or detaches a standard channel.
With \fIchannelId\fR: closes the specified temporary file channel,
or detaches \fBstdin\fR, \fBstdout\fR, or \fBstderr\fR (matching
Tcl 8.x behavior).  The platform may veto the close via the
\fBxCloseTemporaryData\fR callback.
Without arguments: closes all temporary file channels (fail-safe
cleanup), skipping standard channels.
.TP
\fBsource\fR \fIname\fR
Retrieves the contents identified by the opaque name \fIname\fR
via the platform's
.B xGetData
callback, and evaluates the contents as a script.
.PP
The C functions
.BR Th8_Input ,
.BR Th8_Output ,
and
.B Th8_GetData
each take an additional
.I "int flags"
parameter.  The following flag is defined:
.TP
.B TH8_TRANSLATE_EOL (1)
When set on input (via
.BR Th8_Input " or " Th8_GetData ),
\\r\\n sequences are translated to \\n.
When set on output (via
.BR Th8_Output ),
\\n is translated to \\r\\n.
This is used when reading script files that may have DOS line
endings.
.SS "BINARY LOADING"
.TP
\fBload\fR \fIname\fR ?\fIinitProc\fR?
Load the binary extension identified by \fIname\fR into the interpreter.
The platform's
.B xLoad
callback is invoked.  On success, the init function (derived from
\fIname\fR or specified as \fIinitProc\fR) is called, which registers
commands.  The library is recorded for later [unload].
Loading must be enabled by the host via
.BR Th8_EnableLoad .
.TP
\fBunload\fR ?\fB\-nocomplain\fR? ?\fB\-keeplibrary\fR? ?\fB\-nokeeplibrary\fR? ?\fB\-\-\fR? \fIname\fR
Unload a previously loaded binary extension.
The library's _Unload entry point is called (if present), and the
library is removed from the interpreter's tracking list.
.RS
.TP
.B \-keeplibrary
(Default.)  Call the _Unload entry point but do not close
the shared library.  The library remains mapped in the process.
.TP
.B \-nokeeplibrary
Call the _Unload entry point and then close the shared library
via dlclose/FreeLibrary.  This option requires the host to have
enabled
.B TH8_UNLOAD_DANGEROUS
via
.BR Th8_EnableUnload .
.TP
.B \-nocomplain
Suppress all errors silently, returning an empty string.
.TP
.B \-\-
End of options; the next argument is treated as the library name
even if it begins with a dash.
.RE
.IP
Unloading must be enabled by the host via
.BR Th8_EnableUnload .
.SS "FILE COMMANDS"
.TP
\fBfile dirname\fR \fIname\fR
Returns the directory portion of \fIname\fR, omitting the last
component and its preceding separator.  Returns "." if the path
contains no separator.  Pure string manipulation.
.TP
\fBfile exists\fR \fIname\fR
Returns 1 if the named data exists according to the platform's
.B xDataExists
callback, 0 otherwise.  Returns 0 if
.B xDataExists
is not available or if \fIname\fR is the empty string.
.TP
\fBfile join\fR \fIname\fR ?\fIname\fR ...?
Combines path components using a forward-slash separator.
An absolute component resets the result.  Pure string manipulation.
.TP
\fBfile normalize\fR \fIname\fR
Returns the canonical absolute path for \fIname\fR.
Routes through the platform's
.B xNormalizePath
callback, which resolves
.BR . ", " ..
components and makes relative paths absolute.  On POSIX,
.BR realpath (3)
is used for existing paths.  If the platform callback is not
available, the path is returned unchanged.
.TP
\fBfile split\fR \fIname\fR
Returns a list whose elements are the components of the path.
A leading separator becomes the first element.  Pure string manipulation.
.TP
\fBfile tail\fR \fIname\fR
Returns the last component of \fIname\fR (everything after the
last path separator).  Returns the name unchanged if it contains
no separator.  Returns the empty string for root paths.
Pure string manipulation.
.TP
\fBfile extension\fR \fIname\fR
Returns the file extension of \fIname\fR (the last dot and
everything after it in the last path component), or the empty
string if no extension is present.  Pure string manipulation.
.TP
\fBfile nativename\fR \fIname\fR
Converts directory separators to the native form: forward slashes
on POSIX, backslashes on Windows.  Pure string manipulation.
.TP
\fBfile pathtype\fR \fIname\fR
Returns one of \fBabsolute\fR, \fBrelative\fR, or
\fBvolumerelative\fR.  An absolute path begins with a root
separator or drive letter plus separator.  A volumerelative path
begins with a drive letter without a separator (Windows only).
All other paths are relative.  Pure string manipulation.
.TP
\fBfile rootname\fR \fIname\fR
Returns the path with the file extension removed (everything
before the last dot in the last path component).  If there is no
extension, returns the path unchanged.  Pure string manipulation.
.TP
\fBfile rootpath\fR \fIname\fR
Returns the filesystem root or mount point for the specified path
via the platform's \fBxGetRootPath\fR callback.  On POSIX,
this is typically \fB/\fR.  On Windows, this is the volume root
(e.g., \fBC:\\\fR).
.TP
\fBfile same\fR \fIname1\fR \fIname2\fR
Returns non-zero if both paths refer to the exact same physical
file, as determined by the platform's \fBxSameFile\fR callback.
On POSIX, this compares inode and device numbers.  On Windows,
this uses \fBGetFileInformationByHandle\fR for file identity.
.TP
\fBfile separator\fR ?\fIname\fR?
Without arguments, returns the native directory separator
character (\fB/\fR on POSIX, \fB\\\fR on Windows).  With a
\fIname\fR argument, returns the first directory separator
found in the name, or the native separator if none is present.
.TP
\fBfile type\fR \fIname\fR
Returns the type of the file identified by \fIname\fR:
\fBfile\fR for regular files, \fBdirectory\fR for directories,
\fBfile symbolicLink\fR or \fBdirectory symbolicLink\fR for
symbolic links, \fBunsupported\fR for other file types, and
\fBunknown\fR when the native API fails or the file does not
exist.
.TP
\fBfile under\fR \fIname1\fR \fIname2\fR
Returns non-zero if \fIname1\fR resides within the directory
hierarchy of \fIname2\fR.
.TP
\fBfile validname\fR \fIpath\fR ?\fIpathType\fR?
Returns non-zero if \fIpath\fR is syntactically valid for the
operating system.  This command is exempt from base-path
relative path restrictions.  The only supported \fIpathType\fR
value is \fBNone\fR (case-insensitive).
.TP
\fBpwd\fR
Returns the current working directory via the platform's
.B xGetCwd
callback.  In TH8's security model, the result is
.B \(dq.\(dq
(the base directory) or a relative path of the form
.B \(dq./subdir\(dq
if the current directory is underneath the base directory.
Returns an error if the platform does not provide
.BR xGetCwd ,
or if the current directory is outside the base directory.
.TP
\fBcd\fR ?\fIdirName\fR?
Changes the current working directory via the platform's
.B xSetCwd
callback.  If \fIdirName\fR is omitted, it defaults to
.B \(dq.\(dq
(the base directory).  Only paths at or underneath the base
directory are accepted; all other paths produce a
\(lqpermission denied\(rq error.  Returns an empty string on success.
Returns an error if the platform does not provide
.BR xSetCwd .
.SS "TIME AND PROCESS"
.TP
\fBclock seconds\fR
Returns the current time as an integer number of seconds since
an implementation-defined epoch.
Returns
.B 0
if time is unsupported.
.TP
\fBclock ntp\fR ?\fIserver ...\fR?
Queries NTP v4 servers for the current time.  Returns the
Unix timestamp with anti-spoof origin timestamp verification,
multi-server consensus, and backward-clock detection.
Requires the platform's
.B xTimeMs
callback.
.TP
\fBclock https\fR
Retrieves authenticated time from an HTTPS endpoint with
RSA-SHA512 signature verification against the embedded
.B keyTime
public key.  Uses a random nonce for anti-replay protection.
.TP
\fBafter\fR \fImilliseconds\fR
Sleeps for \fImilliseconds\fR via the platform's
.B xSleep
callback.  The sleep is interruptible by
.BR "interp cancel" ;
checking occurs every 50ms.  Returns the empty string.
It is an error if the platform does not provide
.BR xSleep .
.TP
\fBtime\fR \fIscript\fR ?\fIcount\fR?
Evaluates \fIscript\fR \fIcount\fR times (default 1) and returns
a string of the form \(lq\fIN\fR microseconds per iteration\(rq.
Uses the platform's
.B xTimeUs
callback for microsecond resolution.
.TP
\fBpid\fR
Returns the process identifier of the host process.
Returns
.B 0
if unsupported.
.SS "NAMESPACES"
.TP
\fBnamespace eval\fR \fIname\fR \fIscript\fR ?\fIscript ...\fR?
Evaluates \fIscript\fR in namespace \fIname\fR, creating it if
necessary.  Commands defined via
.B proc
during the eval are placed in that namespace.  Multiple script
arguments are concatenated with spaces.
.RS
.PP
.B "Ephemeral frame semantics:"
Variables created by
.B set
inside
.B "namespace eval"
are local to the evaluation frame and are
.B destroyed
when the eval completes.  Only the
.B variable
command creates persistent namespace variables.  This differs from
Tcl 8.x/9.x, where
.B set
inside
.B "namespace eval"
creates persistent namespace variables.  Use
.B variable
for state that must outlive the eval.
.RE
.TP
\fBnamespace current\fR
Returns the fully-qualified name of the current namespace.
.TP
\fBnamespace exists\fR \fIname\fR
Returns
.B 1
if \fIname\fR exists as a namespace,
.B 0
otherwise.
.TP
\fBnamespace children\fR ?\fInamespace\fR?
Returns a list of the fully-qualified names of all child
namespaces of \fInamespace\fR (default: current namespace).
.TP
\fBnamespace parent\fR ?\fInamespace\fR?
Returns the fully-qualified name of the parent of
\fInamespace\fR.  Returns the empty string for the global
namespace.
.TP
\fBnamespace delete\fR \fInamespace\fR ?\fInamespace ...\fR?
Deletes the named namespace(s) and all their contents.  It is
an error to delete the global namespace
.BR :: .
.TP
\fBnamespace code\fR \fIscript\fR
Returns a script that evaluates \fIscript\fR in the current
namespace when invoked later.
.TP
\fBnamespace export\fR ?\fIpattern ...\fR?
Adds glob patterns to the namespace's export list.  Only
commands whose names match an export pattern are eligible for
.BR "namespace import" .
With no arguments, returns the current export list.
.TP
\fBnamespace import\fR ?\fB\-force\fR? \fIpattern ...\fR?
Creates local commands that delegate to exported commands in
another namespace.  Each \fIpattern\fR is a fully qualified
glob (e.g., \fB::srcns::*\fR).  Imported commands execute in
the defining namespace context: \fBnamespace current\fR
returns the source namespace, and the \fBvariable\fR command
accesses the source namespace's variables.
.RS
.PP
If \fB\-force\fR is specified, any existing command in the
target namespace with the same name as an imported command is
silently overwritten.  Without \fB\-force\fR, existing
commands are not replaced.
.RE
.PP
Command resolution: unqualified names are searched first in the
current namespace, then in the global namespace.  Qualified
names (containing
.BR :: )
are resolved directly in the named namespace.
.SS "PACKAGES"
.TP
\fBpackage provide\fR \fIname\fR ?\fIversion\fR?
Declares that package \fIname\fR version \fIversion\fR is loaded.
Without \fIversion\fR, returns the currently loaded version.
.TP
\fBpackage require\fR ?\fB\-exact\fR? \fIname\fR ?\fIversion\fR?
Loads package \fIname\fR.  Evaluates a registered
.B "package ifneeded"
script, or the
.B "package unknown"
handler if no script is registered.
.TP
\fBpackage ifneeded\fR \fIname\fR \fIversion\fR ?\fIscript\fR?
Registers or queries the script that loads \fIname\fR version
\fIversion\fR.
.TP
\fBpackage names\fR
Returns a list of all packages registered via
.BR "package provide" .
.TP
\fBpackage forget\fR \fIname\fR
Removes all record of package \fIname\fR.
.TP
\fBpackage unknown\fR ?\fIcommand\fR?
Gets or sets the handler invoked when
.B "package require"
cannot find a package.
.TP
\fBpackage vcompare\fR \fIv1\fR \fIv2\fR
Returns \-1, 0, or 1 comparing dotted version strings.
.TP
\fBpackage vsatisfies\fR \fIversion\fR \fIrequirement\fR
Returns 1 if \fIversion\fR satisfies \fIrequirement\fR (same
major, remaining components >=).
.SS "EVENTS"
.TP
\fBupdate\fR ?\fB\-idletasks\fR?
Drain the per-interpreter event queue once and return.  Without
\fB\-idletasks\fR, all queued events are processed; with
\fB\-idletasks\fR, only idle-task events are processed.  The
command does not block: when the queue is empty it returns
immediately.  Useful for letting deferred work
.RB ( "after 0" ", " "vwait" -signaled state updates) finish before
proceeding.
.TP
\fBvwait\fR ?\fB\-timeout\fR \fIms\fR? \fIvarName\fR
Block the current call until
.I varName
is written, then return its new value.  Other queued events
continue to be processed while waiting (NRE-aware: nested
.B vwait
calls cooperate correctly).  With
.BR \-timeout ,
raise a
.B "vwait: timeout"
script error if
.I varName
is not signaled within
.I ms
milliseconds; otherwise the wait is unbounded.
.SS "INTERPRETER CONTROL"
.TP
\fBinterp cancel\fR ?\fB\-unwind\fR? ?\fImessage\fR?
Cancels the current interpreter.  Without \fB\-unwind\fR, the
cancellation is interceptable by
.BR catch .
With \fB\-unwind\fR, the cancellation propagates through
.B catch
and unwinds the entire call stack.  If \fImessage\fR is given,
it becomes the error message; otherwise a default message is used.
.SS "SECURITY COMMANDS"
.TP
\fBsecure create\fR \fIvarName\fR ?\fIvalue\fR?
Creates a new secure (AES-256-GCM encrypted-at-rest) variable.
Allocates a key slot, generates a random key, and encrypts the
initial value.  Requires \fBTH8_ENABLE_CRYPTOGRAPHY\fR.
.TP
\fBsecure exists\fR \fIvarName\fR
Returns 1 if \fIvarName\fR is a secure variable, 0 otherwise.
.TP
\fBsecure delete\fR \fIvarName\fR
Securely deletes the variable: zeros the ciphertext, zeros and
frees the key slot, and unsets the Tcl variable.
.TP
\fBflags have\fR \fIflags\fR \fIrequired\fR
Returns 1 if all of the \fIrequired\fR attribute flags are
present in \fIflags\fR, 0 otherwise.  Supports wildcard
characters: \fB*\fR, \fB#\fR, \fB!\fR, \fB$\fR, \fB@\fR.
.TP
\fBflags change\fR \fIflags\fR \fIchanges\fR
Applies flag changes to \fIflags\fR, where \fIchanges\fR is a
list of \fB+flag\fR (add) and \fB\-flag\fR (remove) directives.
Returns the resulting flags string.
.TP
\fBharpy sign\fR \fIpublicKeyToken\fR \fIscriptText\fR
Produce an RSA PKCS#1 v1.5 SHA\-512 detached signature over
.I scriptText
using the private key identified by
.IR publicKeyToken
(a 16\-hex\-digit token matching one of the keys loaded into
the interpreter).  Returns the signature as a base64\-encoded
string in the
.I .b64sig
sidecar format used by
.BR signScript.sh
and the signed\-only policy.  The private key half must be
available in the loaded key material; key entries shipped
with only the public half cannot sign.  Requires
.BR TH8_ENABLE_CRYPTOGRAPHY .
.TP
\fBharpy verify\fR \fIpublicKeyToken\fR \fIscriptText\fR \fIsigText\fR
Verify
.I sigText
(base64) against
.I scriptText
using the public key identified by
.IR publicKeyToken .
Returns 1 on a valid signature, 0 on a verifiable
non\-match.  Raises a script error on malformed input or
when
.I publicKeyToken
does not match the token embedded in the signature header
(token\-mismatch is rejected at parse time so that a
signature meant for one key cannot be silently re\-verified
against another).
.SS "HASHING"
.TP
\fBhash normal\fR \fIalgorithm\fR \fIstring\fR
Computes a cryptographic digest of \fIstring\fR using the
specified \fIalgorithm\fR and returns the hexadecimal result.
The only supported algorithm is \fBSHA512\fR (case-insensitive).
.SS "INTROSPECTION"
.TP
\fBinfo cmdcount\fR
Returns the total number of commands evaluated by the interpreter
since it was created.
.TP
\fBinfo commands\fR ?\fIpattern\fR?
Returns a list of all commands matching the glob \fIpattern\fR.
If \fIpattern\fR is omitted, all commands are listed.
.TP
\fBinfo context\fR
Returns a stable 128-character hexadecimal string that serves as
a SHA-512 fingerprint of the process identity.  The fingerprint
is computed once from a 32-byte seed composed of the parent
process ID (4 bytes), process ID (4 bytes), thread ID (8 bytes),
and 16 bytes of cryptographic random entropy.  The value is
cached for the lifetime of the process.
.TP
\fBinfo globals\fR ?\fIpattern\fR?
Returns a list of all global variables matching \fIpattern\fR.
.TP
\fBinfo exists\fR \fIvarname\fR
Returns
.B 1
if \fIvarname\fR exists in the current scope,
.B 0
otherwise.
.TP
\fBinfo level\fR ?\fInumber\fR?
With no argument, returns the current procedure nesting level
(0\ at global scope).  With an argument, returns a list of the
command name and arguments at that level.  Positive values are
absolute (1\ =\ outermost procedure); zero or negative values
are relative to the current level (0\ =\ current,
\-1\ =\ caller).
.TP
\fBinfo library\fR
Returns
.B ./lib/th8
if the \fBth8\fR package has been provided (via
.BR "package provide th8" ),
or an empty string if it has not.  The check is based on the
package registration state, not the existence of the directory
on disk.  This allows scripts to locate the TH8 script library
directory for sourcing auxiliary files.
.TP
\fBinfo patchlevel\fR
Returns the TH8 patch level version string (e.g., \fB1.0.0\fR).
The value is read from the
.B VERSION
file at build time and stored in
.BR ::tcl_platform(patchLevel) .
The format must conform to the regular expression
.BR {^\ed+\e.\ed+(?:\e.\ed){0,2}(?:-[0-9A-Za-z][_0-9A-Za-z])?$} .
.TP
\fBinfo complete\fR \fIscript\fR
Returns
.B 1
if \fIscript\fR is syntactically complete (all braces, brackets,
and double-quotes are balanced),
.B 0
otherwise.  Useful for interactive shells that need to determine
whether to prompt for continuation input.
.TP
\fBinfo args\fR \fIprocname\fR
Returns a list of the argument names of the procedure
\fIprocname\fR.
.TP
\fBinfo body\fR \fIprocname\fR
Returns the body of the procedure \fIprocname\fR.
.TP
\fBinfo default\fR \fIprocname\fR \fIargname\fR \fIvarname\fR
Returns 1 if the argument \fIargname\fR of procedure \fIprocname\fR
has a default value, storing it in \fIvarname\fR; returns 0
otherwise.  When the argument has no default, \fIvarname\fR is set
to the empty string.  It is an error if \fIprocname\fR does not
exist or \fIargname\fR is not an argument of the procedure.
.TP
\fBinfo functions\fR ?\fIpattern\fR?
Returns a list of all registered math functions matching
\fIpattern\fR.
.TP
\fBinfo loaded\fR
Returns a list of all dynamically loaded libraries in the
interpreter.
.TP
\fBinfo nameofexecutable\fR
Returns the path to the host executable, as determined by the
platform's \fBxGetExePath\fR callback.  Falls back to the
\fB::th8_nameofexecutable\fR variable if set.
.TP
\fBinfo procs\fR ?\fIpattern\fR?
Returns a list of all procedures (defined via
.B proc
or
.BR nproc )
matching \fIpattern\fR.  Built-in commands are excluded.
.TP
\fBinfo script\fR
Returns the name of the innermost currently executing
.B source
file, or the empty string if not in a source context.
.TP
\fBinfo sharedlibextension\fR
Returns the platform-specific shared library extension
(e.g., \fB.so\fR, \fB.dylib\fR, \fB.dll\fR).
.TP
\fBinfo subcommands\fR \fIcommand\fR ?\fIpattern\fR?
Returns a list of all subcommands of the ensemble \fIcommand\fR.
If \fIpattern\fR is given, only subcommand names matching the
glob pattern are returned.  Supported ensembles include
.BR info ", " file ", " string ", " namespace ", " package ", and " array .
It is an error if \fIcommand\fR is not an ensemble.
.TP
\fBinfo varlinks\fR
Returns a list of all variable names in the current call frame
that are linked to variables in other frames via \fBupvar\fR,
\fBglobal\fR, or \fBvariable\fR.
.TP
\fBinfo vars\fR ?\fIpattern\fR?
Returns a list of all variables in the current scope matching
\fIpattern\fR.
.SH "GLOBAL VARIABLES"
The following variables are initialized at interpreter creation:
.TP
.B ::argv
Argument list (empty by default).
.TP
.B ::auto_path
Package index search path.  A Tcl list of directory names that
.B Th8_AutoPathSearch
scans for
.B pkgIndex.th8
files at interpreter startup.  Initialized to
.B "lib/th8 lib/Standard1.0"
by default; embedders may prepend or append paths before calling
.BR Th8_AutoPathSearch .
.TP
.B ::errorCode
Machine-readable error code.
Set to
.B NONE
on each error unless a specific code is provided.
.TP
.B ::errorInfo
Human-readable error stack trace.
Built up during error propagation.
.TP
.B ::tcl_platform(engine)
Engine identifier:
.BR TH8 .
.TP
.B ::tcl_platform(patchLevel)
Patch level version string (e.g., \fB1.0.0\fR).  Read from the
.B VERSION
file at build time.  Also returned by
.BR "info patchlevel" .
The format conforms to
.BR {^\ed+\e.\ed+(?:\e.\ed){0,2}(?:-[0-9A-Za-z][_0-9A-Za-z])?$} .
.TP
.B ::tcl_platform(platform)
Platform identifier:
.B windows
or
.BR unix .
.TP
.B ::tcl_platform(source)
Three-element list:
.BR "{source_id source_timestamp source_tags}" .
Contains the VCS checkout ID (Fossil hash or Git SHA), the
checkout datetime, and the branch/tag names.
Set at build time from
.B "fossil info"
or
.BR "git log" .
.TP
.B ::tcl_platform(host)
The hostname of the machine running the process.
On POSIX, obtained from
.BR gethostname (2).
On Windows, obtained from
.BR GetComputerNameA .
Empty string if the hostname cannot be determined.
.TP
.B ::tcl_platform(user)
The login name of the user running the process.
On POSIX, obtained from
.BR getpwuid (3)
or the
.B USER
environment variable.
On Windows, obtained from
.BR GetUserNameA .
Empty string if the user name cannot be determined.
.TP
.B ::tcl_platform(compileOptions)
A Tcl list of active compile-time options (with the
.B TH8_
prefix stripped).
.TP
.B ::tcl_precision
Controls double-to-string significant digits.
.B 0
means shortest representation.
.SH "SECURITY MODEL"
.SS "Safe by construction"
TH8 has no built-in command that directly accesses the host
operating system.  There is no
.BR open ", " exec ", " socket ", or " file
command.  ALL external interaction \(em I/O, file access, time,
process identity, entropy \(em is mediated by
.B Th8_Platform
callbacks that the embedder provides.
.PP
This means every command is inherently safe: no command can
escape the interpreter sandbox.  The security boundary is the
platform callback table, not a command allowlist.
.PP
.BR gets " and " puts
operate on channels identified by a
.I pChannel
pointer passed to the I/O callbacks; NULL selects the default
channel (stdin/stdout/stderr).  Since there is no
.B open
command, scripts cannot obtain new channel handles.
.BR source
retrieves data through
.BR xGetData ,
which the platform controls (allowlist, virtual filesystem, or
NULL to disable).
.BR "clock seconds"
reads the system clock at one-second granularity (insufficient
for timing attacks); the callback may return a fixed value.
.SS "Base path and file system confinement"
The
.I base path
is the directory from which TH8 operates.  It is determined
automatically from the location of the TH8 shared library at
platform initialization time.  If the shared library resides in
a directory named
.B bin
or
.BR lib ,
the parent directory is used as the base path.
.PP
The string
.B \(dq.\(dq
in all file system operations resolves to the base path.  The
.B xGetCwd
callback returns
.B \(dq.\(dq
if the actual current directory is the base directory, or a
relative path of the form
.B \(dq./subdir\(dq
if it is underneath the base directory, and returns NULL
(producing a \(lqpermission denied\(rq error) if the current
directory is outside the base directory.
.PP
The
.B xSetCwd
callback accepts only paths that resolve to locations at or
underneath the base directory.
.PP
The
.B xNormalizePath
callback returns paths under the base directory in the form
.B \(dq./relative/path\(dq
and returns paths outside the base directory verbatim as
absolute paths.
.PP
If any of these callbacks
.RB ( xGetCwd ", " xSetCwd ", " xNormalizePath )
is NULL, the corresponding script command returns a
\(lqpermission denied: access to file system unavailable\(rq
error.
.SS "Platform lifecycle callbacks"
The
.B Th8_Platform
structure provides
.B xInitialize
and
.B xFinalize
callbacks for platform-level resource management.
.B xInitialize
is called by
.B Th8_Initialize
before any other platform callbacks, allowing the platform to
set up mutexes, heaps, and other process-global resources.
.B xFinalize
is called by
.B Th8_Finalize
after all other cleanup, allowing the platform to release those
resources.  If either callback is NULL, the corresponding step
is silently skipped.
.SS "Step counter"
A per-interpreter counter bounds total work.
Each command invocation, expression evaluation, pattern match
iteration, and string search step increments the counter.
When the configurable limit is exceeded, the interpreter returns
an error:
.IR "step limit exceeded" .
.SS "Result size limit"
A per-interpreter limit bounds the size of any string being
constructed.
When exceeded:
.IR "result size limit exceeded" .
.SS "Memory allocation limit"
A per-interpreter ceiling on total memory allocated through the
platform.  Prevents untrusted scripts from exhausting host memory
via large numbers of variables, namespaces, or strings.
Set via
.BR Th8_SetAllocLimit() .
.SS "Integer overflow protection"
When enabled (default), integer arithmetic in
.B expr
and
.B incr
returns an error on overflow instead of wrapping silently.
Disable via
.B Th8_SetOverflowCheck(interp,\ 0)
for Tcl 8.x compatible wrapping.
.SS "Cancellation"
A running script may be asynchronously cancelled via
.BR Th8_CancelEval() .
The check is performed at every work-unit boundary via the
unified
.B Th8_Ready()
function.  With the
.B TH8_CANCEL_UNWIND
flag,
.B catch
cannot intercept the cancellation error, ensuring it propagates
to the outermost evaluation caller.
.SS "Native stack checking"
If the platform provides stack bounds, TH8 detects impending
C stack overflow at every recursive entry point.
.SS "Taint tracking"
Strings originating from untrusted sources carry a taint bit.
Tainted strings cannot be evaluated as scripts.
.SS "Pre-evaluation callback"
The host may register a callback invoked before every script
evaluation, which can reject scripts.  The callback receives the
script text, the script origin name (if known), and the context
pointer.  The origin name (zName/nName) is supplied by the
evaluation functions
.RB ( Th8_Eval ", " Th8_EvalDownlevel ", " Th8_Expr ", " Th8_NREval )
and is typically the file name from which the script was loaded.
If the callback returns anything other than
.BR TH8_OK ,
evaluation is aborted with that return code.
.SS "Platform threading and diagnostic callbacks"
.TP
\fBxIntCmpXchg\fR(\fIpTarget\fR, \fIiExchange\fR, \fIiComparand\fR)
Atomic integer compare-and-exchange.  Atomically compares
\fI*pTarget\fR with \fIiComparand\fR; if equal, stores
\fIiExchange\fR.  Returns the original value.
On Windows, maps to \fBInterlockedCompareExchange\fR.
On POSIX, maps to \fB__sync_val_compare_and_swap\fR.
.TP
\fBxMemBarrier\fR()
Full memory barrier.  Ensures all prior writes are visible
to other threads.  On Windows, maps to \fBMemoryBarrier()\fR.
On POSIX, maps to \fB__sync_synchronize()\fR.
.TP
\fBxEmitTrace\fR(\fIzMsg\fR, \fIpCtx\fR)
Emit a diagnostic trace message.  Used to report platform
errors such as initialization failures.  On Windows, calls
\fBOutputDebugStringA\fR.  On POSIX, calls \fBsyslog()\fR.
.SH "C API REFERENCE"
The complete C programming interface is documented in
.BR th8_api (3).
.SH "SCRIPT ANNOTATIONS"
Scripts may contain annotations in Tcl comments that control
when and how the script may be executed:
.TP
.B "# <<notBefore:YYYY_MM_DDThh_mm_ssZ>>"
Earliest valid execution time.  The timestamp uses strict
ISO-8601 format with underscore delimiters (Eagle compatibility).
.TP
.B "# <<notAfter:YYYY_MM_DDThh_mm_ssZ>>"
Latest valid execution time.
.TP
.B "# <<flags:attributeFlags>>"
Attribute flags (no spaces; complex flag strings are permitted).
.PP
Annotations are extracted from all scripts when the policy
callbacks are installed.  The parsed values populate the
.B ::th8_security(notBefore)
,
.B ::th8_security(notAfter)
, and
.B ::th8_security(flags)
array elements.
Enforcement (rejection of expired or not-yet-valid scripts)
occurs only when the signed-only policy is enabled.
Timestamps are validated with strict ISO-8601 rules including
leap year bounds.
.SH "SCRIPT SIGNATURE VERIFICATION"
Script signature verification and RSA key management are documented in
.BR th8_api (3).
.SH "EMBEDDED PUBLIC KEYS"
Embedded public key management is documented in
.BR th8_api (3).
.SH "COMPARISON WITH OTHER IMPLEMENTATIONS"
.SS "TH8 vs TH1"
TH1 is the minimal Tcl interpreter embedded in Fossil.
TH8 is a ground-up redesign sharing no code with TH1.
TH1 has a flat command set with no namespaces, packages, or
expressions beyond basic integer arithmetic.
TH8 provides the full Tcl 8.4 expression evaluator (28 math
functions, all operators, ternary), namespaces with qualified
name resolution, the package system, NRE (non-recursive engine),
and a platform abstraction layer.
TH1 has no security hardening; TH8 has overflow-checked
arithmetic, SipHash, step/memory limits, and safe-by-construction
command set.
.SS "TH8 vs Tcl (native)"
Native Tcl (8.x/9.x) is a full-featured language with bytecode
compilation, event loops, channel I/O, socket networking, regular
expression extensions, and a large standard library.
TH8 implements the Tcl 8.4 core language (lexical rules,
substitution, expressions, procedures, namespaces, packages) but
intentionally omits features that create host dependencies:
.BR open ", " exec ", " socket ", " file ", " encoding ", "
.BR interp " (multi-interp), " chan ", and event loops."
This makes TH8 suitable for embedding in security-critical
environments where native Tcl's host access would be a liability.
TH8's platform abstraction has no equivalent in native Tcl,
which calls the C runtime and OS APIs directly.
.SS "TH8 vs Eagle"
Eagle is a managed (CLR/.NET) implementation of the Tcl language
with extensions for .NET interop, COM automation, and Windows
integration.  Eagle provides a compatible command set with
additional features like the
.B nproc
(named parameters) and
.B downlevel
commands, which TH8 also implements.
TH8 and Eagle share design philosophy (security-first, platform
abstraction) but differ in implementation: Eagle runs on the CLR,
TH8 is portable C99.  Both are intended to be conforming
implementations of the Tcl Language Standard.
.SS "TH8 vs Jim Tcl"
Jim Tcl is a small Tcl interpreter focused on embedded use.
Like TH8, it is written in C and targets resource-constrained
environments.  Jim implements a subset of Tcl 8.5+ including
.B {*}
expansion and some features TH8 omits (event loop, I/O channels,
.BR dict ).
TH8 differs by using Tcl 8.4 syntax (no
.BR {*} ),
providing a full platform abstraction layer (Jim calls the CRT
directly), NRE-based non-recursive evaluation, and comprehensive
security hardening (overflow checking, SipHash, memory limits).
Jim has no equivalent to TH8's safe-by-construction command model.
.SS "TH8 vs Picol"
Picol is a tiny (~500 line) Tcl interpreter by Salvatore
Sanfilippo (antirez).  It demonstrates that a working Tcl core
can be extremely small.
TH8 is significantly more complete: full expression evaluator,
namespaces, packages, NRE, regexp, security hardening, and a
platform abstraction layer.  Picol is a teaching tool; TH8 is
production-grade.
.SS "TH8 vs Jacl"
Jacl (Java Command Language) is a Java implementation of Tcl.
It provides Tcl semantics on the JVM, similar to how Eagle
provides them on the CLR.
TH8 targets bare-metal and embedded environments where a JVM
is unavailable.  Jacl has no platform abstraction; it relies
on Java's standard library for all I/O.  TH8's C99 implementation
with zero CRT dependencies makes it suitable for UEFI, kernel,
and IoT use cases that Jacl cannot serve.
.SH "OPTIONAL DEPENDENCIES"
.SS "DNSSEC validation (libunbound)"
When built with
.B TH8_ENABLE_UNBOUND
(the default on systems where libunbound is available),
TH8 uses libunbound for DNSSEC validation of DNS queries
made by
.B "[clock ntp]"
and the libcurl data-retrieval layer.
.PP
On macOS with Homebrew:
.RS
.nf
brew install unbound
sudo /opt/homebrew/sbin/unbound\-anchor \\
    \-a /opt/homebrew/etc/unbound/root.key
sudo mkdir \-p /etc/unbound
sudo ln \-s /opt/homebrew/etc/unbound/root.key \\
    /etc/unbound/root.key
.fi
.RE
.PP
The symlink is required because libunbound's validator module
looks for the IANA root trust anchor at
.B /etc/unbound/root.key
regardless of Homebrew's install prefix.
Without it, harmless but noisy diagnostic messages are emitted
to stderr on every DNS resolution.
.PP
On Debian/Ubuntu:
.B "apt install libunbound\-dev"
(root.key is installed automatically).
On FreeBSD:
.BR "pkg install unbound" .
